-- title:
--   Specialization pushes the polar divisor of j to ordᵥ(jmath̄)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a level $N \neq 0$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$. Let `data` consist of a monic polynomial $\Phi$ over $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the $q$-expansion data at level $q$, let `hKr` assert the Kronecker congruence $\Phi \bmod q = (C X^{q} - X)(C X - X^{q})$, and let `hα`, `hβ` assert that the ring homomorphisms $\overline{\alpha}$, $\overline{\beta}$ attached to the Hecke correspondence at level $N$ and prime $q$ are integral. Assume $q \nmid N$. Let $P$ be a place specialization in this situation, so in particular $P$ provides a map $P.\mathrm{sp}$ from places of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \subset$ `modularFunctionFieldBar N` (the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full modular function field of level $N$ inside Laurent series) to places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, the subfield of $k$-Laurent series generated by $\bar\jmath$ and $\bar\jmath_N$, together with its compatibilities and its map on degree-zero divisor classes; and let $R$ be a prolongation tuple over $P$ satisfying `IsModel`, i.e. the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws. Let $Dj$ be the finitely supported function on places of `modularFunctionFieldBar N` whose value at $b$ is $\operatorname{ord}_b$ of the element $j$, given by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` under $\mathbb{Q} \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Finally let $v$ be a place of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` which is not an affine geometric place, that is, the two generators `jGeomGen k N` and `jNGeomGen k N` do not both lie in the valuation subring of $v$. Then the push-forward along $P.\mathrm{sp}$ of the part of $Dj$ supported where $Dj b < 0$, evaluated at $v$, equals $\operatorname{ord}_v$ of the element `jqModC k` of `modularFunctionFieldC k N`: the orders of $j$ at those of its poles which specialize to $v$ sum to the order of $\bar\jmath$ at $v$.
--
--   In classical terms this says that the widths of the cusps of $X_0(N)$ specializing to a given non-affine (i.e. cuspidal) place of the characteristic-$q$ fibre add up to the width of that place, the model laws of the prolongation tuple being what forbids specializations pairing cusps of differing widths. It is used in the construction of divisors with one-sided first-law behaviour attached to modular units, within the comparison of the level-$N$ curve with its reduction at $q$.
